# Git line-ending policy. Combined with `.editorconfig`, this keeps
# files consistent regardless of the OS the developer uses.
#
# Rationale: Rust, JS/TS, JSON, YAML, and Markdown all treat LF as the
# canonical line ending. CRLF in the repo produces noisy diffs and
# breaks the `cargo fmt --check` and `prettier --check` CI gates that
# run on Linux.

# Default for everything: text, LF on commit, LF in working tree.
*                 text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must always be LF — Windows-style line endings will
# break the shebang and turn them into non-executable garbage on *nix.
*.sh              text eol=lf
*.bash            text eol=lf
*.ps1             text eol=lf
*.bat             text eol=lf

# Markdown hard breaks depend on trailing spaces — leave them alone.
*.md              text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space

# Lockfiles are machine-generated, never edit them by hand.
pnpm-lock.yaml    -text
Cargo.lock        -text
